{"entries":{"chapter-62-study-cerebrovascular-disease|Chapter 62 Study: Cerebrovascular Disease":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Determine ischemic mechanism: large-artery, lacunar, cardioembolic, cryptogenic or other. Etiology guides treatment and prevention (p954).":["u3"],"copy|Obtain urgent brain and vascular imaging. Noncontrast CT rapidly excludes hemorrhage; a negative early CT does not exclude infarction (p953–954).":["u4"],"copy|MRI diffusion imaging is more sensitive for acute infarction; CTA/MRA identifies major arterial disease and large-vessel occlusion (p953, p965).":["u5"],"copy|In eligible patients, IV alteplase is 0.9 mg/kg up to 90 mg total. Give 10% as a bolus over one minute and the remainder over 60 minutes. Review hemorrhage risk and exclusions (p966, Activase prescribing information).":["u6"],"copy|Earlier treatment is better. The chapter supports the selected 3–4.5-hour window and imaging-based selection for some wake-up/extended-window presentations (p967).":["u7"],"copy|Thrombectomy benefits selected disabling large-vessel occlusions; imaging-selected DAWN patients were 6–24 hours from last-known-well. Favorable tissue imaging is not a reason to delay treatment (p968–969).":["u8"],"copy|After IV thrombolysis, maintain BP below 180/105 mm Hg for at least 24 hours and obtain follow-up CT/MRI; antithrombotics are generally withheld until hemorrhage is excluded (p969).":["u9"],"copy|Aspirin 50–325 mg/day is commonly used after ischemic stroke/TIA when appropriate; consider bleeding history and the post-thrombolysis restriction (p970–971).":["u10"],"copy|Match antithrombotic treatment to etiology; routine anticoagulation for embolic stroke of undetermined source did not improve outcomes and increased bleeding in the trials summarized (p974).":["u11"],"copy|Long-term aspirin/clopidogrel combination after lacunar stroke increased bleeding and death without superior stroke prevention (p973).":["u12"],"copy|Control vascular risks; the Treat Stroke to Target trial supports LDL below 70 mg/dL after recent stroke/TIA with atherosclerotic disease (p975).":["u13"],"copy|Deep hemorrhage commonly reflects hypertension; lobar microbleeds suggest cerebral amyloid angiopathy. Imaging, not symptoms alone, distinguishes hemorrhage from infarction (p975–976).":["u14"],"copy|Suspected subarachnoid hemorrhage starts with CT; persistent suspicion after negative CT requires lumbar puncture (p978).":["u15"],"copy|Oral nimodipine 60 mg every 4 hours after SAH reduces delayed ischemia and mortality in the cited trials (p978).":["u16"],"copy|Age alone does not negate reperfusion benefit; disabling deficits and eligibility matter. Incorporate rehabilitation, symptom control and palliative goals throughout care (p968–969, p978).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|2023-Jun Q17":["u19"],"copy|CHAPTER 62s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 62 - Cerebrovascular Disease; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Excluded from the IMA required reading list (P005-2026); cited once in past papers.":["u22"],"copy|Mechanism & diagnosis":["u23"],"copy|Acute ischemic treatment":["u24"],"copy|Secondary prevention":["u25"],"copy|Hemorrhage & exam traps":["u26"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (1):":["u27"],"copy|P005-2026 excludes Hazzard Chapter 62 from required reading, although past examinations include cerebrovascular questions and cite this chapter.":["u22"],"copy|Past-exam example on delayed hydrocephalus after subarachnoid hemorrhage:":["u27"],"what-the-exams-asked|What the exams asked":["u28"],"copy|Localize leg-predominant weakness, loss of initiative and urinary incontinence to the ACA — June 2023 Q76 and June 2022 Q44. The 2023 bibliography specifies Hazzard p958 and handbook p17. The handbook's ACA discussion is on p6.":["u29"],"copy|Distinguish right MCA neglect from a dominant left MCA language deficit — December 2021 Q86, June 2022 Q83, and June 2025 Q72. The 2025 question cites the stroke rehabilitation handbook.":["u30"],"copy|Classify speech from fluency, comprehension and repetition — June 2023 Q5 and September 2024 Q48, both from the handbook. Distinguish apraxia from a spatial or sensory deficit in May 2024 Q69, an atlas question with no printed book page.":["u31"],"copy|Pair ipsilateral abducens palsy with contralateral hemiparesis to identify the pons — June 2023 Q48, handbook p17.":["u32"],"copy|Distinguish arterial plaque embolism from intrinsic perforator disease — December 2021 Q1. Calculate ABCD² from measured blood pressure and the actual TIA deficit — June 2022 Q91.":["u33"],"copy|Investigate recurrent ischemic stroke with persistent unexplained thrombocytopenia — June 2026 Basic Q107. The accompanying Table 96-3 explicitly lists antiphospholipid syndrome.":["u34"],"copy|Recognize cerebral amyloid angiopathy in recurrent lobar hemorrhage — December 2021 Q30. Distinguish acute poststroke seizures from later epilepsy — May 2024 Q18, from Harrison.":["u35"],"copy|Recognize delayed communicating hydrocephalus after subarachnoid hemorrhage — June 2023 Q17.":["u36"],"localizing-weakness-neglect-and-language-impairment|Localizing weakness, neglect and language impairment":["u37"],"choosing-an-ischemic-mechanism-and-estimating-early-risk|Choosing an ischemic mechanism and estimating early risk":["u38"],"hemorrhage-seizures-and-deterioration-during-recovery|Hemorrhage, seizures and deterioration during recovery":["u39"],"reperfusion-prevention-and-recovery|Reperfusion, prevention and recovery":["u40"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u41"],"copy|June 2025 Q11 asks about levodopa.":["u42"],"copy|Its bibliography names Chapter 61 and prints both pp943 and 964.":["u43"],"copy|The Parkinson treatment passage explains the motor complications.":["u44"],"copy|The second number falls in this chapter on other causes of stroke.":["u45"],"copy|Start with the distribution of the deficit.":["u46"],"copy|ACA ischemia affects the medial frontal lobe.":["u47"],"copy|Contralateral leg weakness usually exceeds arm weakness, and bilateral medial frontal injury can impair both legs, bladder control and initiation.":["u48"],"copy|A patient who seems apathetic as well as weak may have abulia, a marked reduction in self-initiated behavior.":["u49"],"copy|A patent anterior communicating artery may protect against proximal occlusion.":["u50"],"copy|An anatomical variant in which one A1 supplies both distal territories makes bilateral injury possible after a single occlusion (p958).":["u51"],"copy|An MCA stem occlusion may combine dense contralateral weakness and sensory loss with a gaze preference toward the injured hemisphere.":["u52"],"copy|In a smaller cortical branch infarct, the face and arm may be much weaker than the leg.":["u53"],"copy|Superior division injury on the language-dominant side can leave comprehension relatively intact while speech becomes effortful.":["u54"],"copy|Inferior division injury may produce fluent speech with poor comprehension and little weakness.":["u55"],"copy|In a right-handed patient, a large right MCA stroke more often disrupts attention to the left than language.":["u56"],"copy|A homonymous visual field defect can occur after an MCA stroke on either side.":["u57"],"copy|Neglect is failure to attend, even when visual or tactile input is detectable (pp955–958, Figures 62-1 and 62-2).":["u58"],"copy|At the bedside, ask for a spontaneous sentence, give a simple command, and ask the patient to repeat a phrase.":["u59"],"copy|Halting speech with reasonably preserved comprehension and poor repetition fits Broca aphasia.":["u60"],"copy|Fluent speech with preserved comprehension but strikingly poor repetition points toward conduction aphasia.":["u61"],"copy|Wernicke aphasia impairs comprehension despite fluent output.":["u62"],"copy|In global aphasia, both speech production and comprehension are severely affected.":["u63"],"copy|A single invented word carries less localizing weight than the whole language examination (Stroke Rehabilitation Clinician Handbook 2020, ch. 1, pp11–14).":["u64"],"copy|The May 2024 CT question tests dominant-hemisphere apraxia.":["u65"],"copy|Ask the person to pantomime using a familiar tool, allowing for weakness, comprehension and sensation.":["u66"],"copy|Someone who knows what is being asked may still be unable to organize the learned gesture.":["u67"],"copy|Anosognosia means unawareness of a deficit.":["u68"],"copy|Astereognosis means inability to identify a familiar object by touch despite preserved elementary sensation.":["u69"],"copy|Neither is synonymous with apraxia (May 2024 atlas, Figure 5, handbook pp9–11, 15).":["u70"],"copy|A deficit on one side of the face or in one cranial nerve combined with weakness or sensory loss in the opposite limbs suggests a brainstem lesion.":["u71"],"copy|Ipsilateral failure of abduction and contralateral weakness localize to the lower pons.":["u72"],"copy|An ipsilateral third-nerve palsy with contralateral weakness points higher, to the midbrain.":["u73"],"copy|Vertigo, dysphagia, hoarseness, ipsilateral facial and contralateral body sensory changes suggest lateral medullary ischemia.":["u74"],"copy|Occipital PCA infarction instead produces a contralateral homonymous field defect (pp959–960, handbook p17).":["u75"],"copy|The same abrupt deficit can follow local small-vessel occlusion, a thrombus shed from an upstream plaque, or a cardiac embolus.":["u76"],"copy|CT or MRI identifies the distribution of injury.":["u77"],"copy|Head and neck vascular imaging identifies stenosis or occlusion, while ECG, rhythm monitoring and selected echocardiography assess a cardiac source.":["u78"],"copy|A transient deficit with a corresponding acute DWI lesion constitutes an infarct under the tissue-based definition.":["u79"],"copy|Resolution of symptoms calls for urgent evaluation because recurrent risk is concentrated early (pp953–957, 964–966).":["u80"],"copy|Artery-to-artery embolism means thrombus forms on diseased arterial endothelium and travels downstream.":["u81"],"copy|An ulcerated internal carotid plaque can embolize at 50–70% stenosis, although this is less common than with severe stenosis.":["u82"],"copy|Hypoperfusion requires inadequate collateral flow and may produce border-zone injury.":["u83"],"copy|A lacune is a small deep noncortical infarct in a perforator territory.":["u84"],"copy|Lipohyalinosis or atheroma near the perforator origin is usual.":["u85"],"copy|Embolism can occur.":["u86"],"copy|Pure motor or pure sensory syndromes without aphasia, neglect or a cortical field cut support the small-vessel mechanism (pp957–961).":["u87"],"copy|Cardioembolic deficits can be maximal at onset and affect more than one vascular territory.":["u88"],"copy|Atrial fibrillation is a major cause in older adults.":["u89"],"copy|A patent foramen ovale is common enough to be incidental.":["u90"],"copy|A venous embolus can cross it, and thrombus can also arise at the opening.":["u91"],"copy|A cryptogenic stroke warrants assessment of the cervical and intracranial vessels and heart rhythm before assigning a mechanism.":["u92"],"copy|Selected patients benefit from longer rhythm monitoring for intermittent AF (pp961–965).":["u93"],"copy|ABCD² assigns one point for age at least 60, one for presenting systolic pressure at least 140 or diastolic at least 90, two for unilateral weakness or one for speech disturbance without weakness, one for a duration of 10–59 minutes or two for at least 60 minutes, and one for diabetes.":["u94"],"copy|The four June 2022 Q91 scenarios score 3, 7, 4 and 5 in order.":["u95"],"copy|The second and fourth received credit, although only the second reaches 7.":["u96"],"copy|The patient's measured pressure matters more here than a history of hypertension.":["u97"],"copy|Do not use a low result to defer assessment (p957, Johnston et al., Lancet 2007, 369:283–292, NICE NG128, recommendation 1.1.6).":["u98"],"copy|In an otherwise unexplained recurrent arterial stroke, a persistent low platelet count can suggest an acquired prothrombotic disorder.":["u99"],"copy|Hazzard includes antiphospholipid antibody testing in selected cryptogenic strokes and lists antiphospholipid syndrome among causes of thrombocytopenia.":["u100"],"copy|Palpitations instead suggest rhythm monitoring, while fever and systemic signs may prompt an endocarditis evaluation.":["u101"],"copy|Other less common mechanisms include arterial dissection, giant cell arteritis, other vasculitides, cerebral venous thrombosis, cancer-associated thrombosis and hereditary arteriopathies.":["u102"],"copy|The accompanying symptoms and the pattern on vessel imaging guide investigation (pp962–965, 971–974, Table 96-3 p1513).":["u103"],"copy|Deep hemorrhage most commonly reflects hypertensive disease of small penetrating arteries in the putamen, thalamus, pons or cerebellum.":["u104"],"copy|Recurrent spontaneous lobar hemorrhage in an older adult instead suggests amyloid deposition in cortical and leptomeningeal vessels.":["u105"],"copy|Susceptibility-weighted MRI can show further small lobar bleeds.":["u106"],"copy|A hemorrhagic metastasis or malformation remains possible when the history and imaging suggest it.":["u107"],"copy|Sudden onset alone cannot distinguish ischemia from bleeding.":["u108"],"copy|Obtain urgent brain imaging (pp975–977).":["u109"],"copy|An acute seizure can complicate a cortical infarct or a hemorrhage.":["u110"],"copy|A population-based study recorded clinical seizures within 24 hours in 8.4% of hemorrhagic and 2.4% of ischemic strokes.":["u111"],"copy|Harrison's comparison uses embolic stroke as a subgroup.":["u112"],"copy|Degenerative disease, including Alzheimer disease, is another cause in later life.":["u113"],"copy|A seizure soon after stroke and unprovoked epilepsy months later have different time courses.":["u114"],"copy|Treat a documented seizure.":["u115"],"copy|Routine primary antiseizure prophylaxis after spontaneous ICH is generally unnecessary (p976, Harrison 21e p3310, Szaflarski et al., Epilepsia 2008, 49:974–981).":["u116"],"copy|A ruptured aneurysm may cause a sudden severe headache, vomiting, neck stiffness or reduced consciousness.":["u117"],"copy|Noncontrast CT is the initial study.":["u118"],"copy|Hazzard discusses lumbar puncture when CT is negative and suspicion persists.":["u119"],"copy|Contemporary NICE guidance distinguishes a negative CT read by a radiologist within six hours, when LP is not routinely offered, from a later negative CT, when LP should be considered (pp977–978, NICE NG228, recommendations 1.1.11–1.1.14).":["u120"],"copy|In a deteriorating survivor of SAH, timing helps choose what to investigate.":["u121"],"copy|Rerupture is especially concerning early.":["u122"],"copy|Vasospasm often develops between days 4 and 14 and may cause a new focal deficit.":["u123"],"copy|Delayed hydrocephalus may appear days to weeks later, with worsening gait, cognition, continence or alertness.":["u124"],"copy|Compare ventricular size on current and earlier scans.":["u125"],"copy|For a safe communicating pattern, temporary CSF removal can help assess whether lasting diversion is likely to help.":["u126"],"copy|Obstruction or acute deterioration calls for urgent specialist assessment of the drainage route (p978, NICE NG228, recommendations 1.3.3–1.3.5).":["u127"],"copy|Rapid noncontrast CT excludes hemorrhage and can identify a large established infarct.":["u128"],"copy|CTA identifies a potentially treatable large-vessel occlusion.":["u129"],"copy|DWI is sensitive to early infarction, and perfusion imaging can help distinguish irreversibly injured core from salvageable penumbra in selected late or unknown-onset presentations.":["u130"],"copy|History must establish when the patient was last known well, what disability remains, baseline function, anticoagulant exposure and bleeding risks.":["u131"],"copy|In an otherwise eligible patient, advanced age alone is insufficient reason to withhold reperfusion (pp953–955, 964–969).":["u132"],"copy|Earlier IV thrombolysis offers more opportunity to preserve function.":["u133"],"copy|Hazzard describes alteplase within the standard 4.5-hour window and separate imaging-selected extended-window trials.":["u134"],"copy|For thrombectomy, vessel location, disabling deficit, infarct volume and salvageable tissue determine eligibility.":["u135"],"copy|DAWN evaluated carefully selected anterior circulation occlusions 6–24 hours from last known well.":["u136"],"copy|The deadline is a limit for eligibility, never a reason to wait.":["u137"],"copy|Worsening headache, reduced alertness or a new deficit after a lytic requires immediate evaluation for intracranial bleeding, even before the routine follow-up scan (pp966–970).":["u138"],"copy|For secondary prevention, match treatment to the mechanism.":["u139"],"copy|Antiplatelets are generally appropriate for noncardioembolic disease.":["u140"],"copy|Short-term dual therapy can reduce early recurrence after selected minor ischemic strokes or high-risk TIAs, but bleeding risk rises with prolonged treatment.":["u141"],"copy|A patient with AF usually needs anticoagulation when safe after infarction.":["u142"],"copy|Empiric anticoagulation for embolic stroke of undetermined source failed to outperform aspirin.":["u143"],"copy|NAVIGATE ESUS found excess major bleeding with rivaroxaban.":["u144"],"copy|Severe symptomatic carotid stenosis warrants prompt assessment for revascularization.":["u145"],"copy|Intracranial stenosis generally starts with intensive medical management.":["u146"],"copy|Blood pressure control, statin therapy where indicated, smoking cessation, exercise and diabetes care address recurrence across mechanisms (pp970–975).":["u147"],"copy|In large hemispheric or cerebellar infarcts, edema can threaten life.":["u148"],"copy|Declining consciousness, brainstem compression or hydrocephalus warrants urgent reassessment and consideration of neurosurgical decompression in selected patients.":["u149"],"copy|In ICH, assess hematoma location, mass effect and anticoagulant exposure promptly.":["u150"],"copy|A stable repeat scan does not neutralize warfarin.":["u151"],"copy|IV vitamin K supports lasting reversal, while four-factor PCC rapidly replaces deficient clotting factors.":["u152"],"copy|Clarify the patient's pre-stroke function, goals and acceptable outcomes when discussing decompression, ventilatory support, feeding and rehabilitation (pp974, 976–979).":["u153"],"copy|Plan recovery around the tasks that matter to the patient, with physical, occupational and speech-language assessment as indicated.":["u154"],"copy|Swallowing problems, aspiration, immobility, pressure injury, spasticity, shoulder problems and depression interfere with progress.":["u155"],"copy|Team-based rehabilitation should begin when medically appropriate, with intensity adapted to the person.":["u156"],"copy|High-dose mobilization in the first 24 hours can worsen outcomes.":["u157"],"copy|Recovery may continue for months.":["u158"],"copy|Routine fluoxetine for motor recovery failed to improve functional outcome in later trials and increased fracture risk (pp965–966, 975, AHA/ASA acute ischemic stroke guideline 2026, Rehabilitation).":["u159"],"copy|זיהוי ופינוי.":["u160"],"copy|הופעה חדשה של קושי בדיבור, חולשה בצד אחד או בלבול מצדיקה פנייה דחופה להערכה רפואית.":["u161"],"copy|גם אם ההפרעה חלפה, משרד הבריאות מפנה לבירור במיון בחשד לאירוע מוחי חולף.":["u162"],"copy|בחשד לשבץ יש לפעול לפינוי מהיר באמבולנס.":["u163"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, ״שבץ מוחי״, 09.10.2024, ״אירוע מוחי חולף״, ״סימנים קריטיים שמעידים על שבץ מוחי״ ו״טיפול ראשוני בשבץ מוחי״.":["u164"],"copy|שיקום לאחר האשפוז.":["u165"],"copy|פגיעה נוירולוגית שמגבילה תפקוד, כגון חולשת יד, עשויה להצדיק שיקום באשפוז.":["u166"],"copy|כשהתסמינים קלים אפשר לעיתים להשתקם בבית.":["u167"],"copy|טיפול של קלינאי תקשורת לאחר שבץ כלול בסל הבריאות.":["u168"],"copy|במגבלה תפקודית משמעותית צוות המרפאה יכול להפנות לריפוי בעיסוק, לפיזיותרפיה ולשירותים נוספים לפי הצורך.":["u169"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, ״שבץ מוחי״, 09.10.2024, ״טיפול בשבץ מוחי איסכמי״ ו״זכויות סוציאליות לאחר שבץ מוחי״.":["u170"],"copy|Within 24 hours of onset, 8.4% of patients with hemorrhagic stroke and 2.9% of those with cerebral infarction had a seizure.":["u111"],"copy|Pooling infarction with transient ischemic attack lowered the latter figure to 2.4%.":["u111"],"copy|Routine primary antiseizure prophylaxis after spontaneous ICH is generally unnecessary (p976, Harrison 21e p3310, Szaflarski et al., Table 2).":["u116"]},"maxId":170}
